I think to get more variety this pool would have to consist in the greatest numbers. Unfortunately, one question for OP today, since you're dividing areas up into square roots in my diagram. Could someone please provide some reference material specifically relating to finding the number of tetrahedral numbers?

Certainly! Tetrahedral numbers are a type of figurate number that represents the number of dots in a triangular array with one layer more than the number of dots in the previous layer.

To calculate the \( n \)-th tetrahedral number \( T_n \), you would use the formula:

\[ T_n = \frac{n(n+1)(n+2)}{6} \]

For example, the first few tetrahedral numbers are:

- \( T_1 \) is the number of dots in one layer.
- \( T_2 \) is the number of dots in two layers.
- \( T_3 \) is the number of dots in three layers.
- And so on.

The highest layer of our tetrahedral number is 5 because the diagram shows 4 dots per layer:

! [Diagram of the tetrahedral 14](https://iimg.com/yenptr/11494/596/21w42t58a-1.jpg)

So if we use 5 total bases, 15 points along three directions, and 3 line segments connecting each point, the points on each relative edge point would simply be able to create three essentially 'zigzagging' tetra numbers.

